> You are right: with five bootstrap forecasts, the object being evaluated is a **predictive distribution**. Your current density score is directionally sensible, but with only five draws, KDE with `bw="SJ"` is unstable—and evaluating density at `low` and `high` is not interval probability.
> 
> ## First: fix a likely LASSO bootstrap problem
> 
> You should create the horizon-shifted target **before** resampling. Currently, bootstrapping rows before shifting `yy` destroys temporal alignment.
> 
> ```julia
> base = copy(lassodf)
> target = "x$xx"
> base[!, :yy] = vcat(base[1+nday:end, target], fill(missing, nday))
> dropmissing!(base, [:yy])
> 
> for bs in 1:25  # ideally more than 5 for a distribution
>     indices = sample(1:nrow(base), nrow(base), replace=true)
>     bootdf = base[indices, :]
>     m = fit(LassoModel, eval(formulaexpr), bootdf)
>     push!(allboots, predict(m, preddf)[1])
> end
> ```
> 
> Five draws are adequate for a rough ensemble, but 25–100 will make distributional scoring and diagnostics much more meaningful.
> 
> ## Replace the R score with an interval log score
> 
> Treat the bootstrap predictions as a log-normal predictive distribution. Score the probability that it assigns to the subsequently observed `[low, high]` range.
> 
> ```r
> interval_logscore <- function(predprice, low, high, sigma_floor=0.01) {
>   x <- log(predprice[is.finite(predprice) & predprice > 0])
>   if (length(x) < 2 || !is.finite(low) || !is.finite(high) || low <= 0 || high <= 0)
>     return(NA_real_)
> 
>   mu <- mean(x)
>   sigma <- max(sd(x), sigma_floor)
> 
>   p <- pnorm(log(max(low, high)), mu, sigma) -
>        pnorm(log(min(low, high)), mu, sigma)
> 
>   log(max(p, 1e-12))
> }
> ```
> 
> Then replace the `density(...)` summary with:
> 
> ```r
> rows2 <- rows %>%
>   group_by(Algorithm, projdate, ahead) %>%
>   summarize(
>     npreds = sum(is.finite(value)),
>     logskill = interval_logscore(value, first(low), first(high)),
>     .groups = "drop"
>   ) %>%
>   filter(npreds >= 5, is.finite(logskill))
> ```
> 
> Your later recency-weighted aggregation can remain conceptually unchanged. Higher `logskill` is better, and it remains an exclusively empirical basis for weights.
